<section>
  <h2>Worked example</h2>
  <p>
    A uniform ladder of mass 10 kg and length 4 m rests with one end on
    rough horizontal ground and the other against a smooth vertical wall,
    at 60° to the ground. Take <i>g</i> = 9.81 m/s². Find the reactions and
    the least coefficient of friction at the ground for which the ladder
    can rest.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Vertical: <i>N</i> = 10 × 9.81 = 98.10 N</li>
    <li>Moments about the foot: <i>S</i> × 4 sin 60° = 98.10 × 2 cos 60°</li>
    <li>4 × 0.8660 <i>S</i> = 98.10 × 1, so 3.464 <i>S</i> = 98.10</li>
    <li><i>S</i> = 28.32 N, and <i>F</i> = <i>S</i> = 28.32 N</li>
    <li>Least <i>μ</i> = <i>F</i>/<i>N</i> = 28.32 / 98.10 = 0.289</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The coefficient of friction at the ground is 0.40. Show that the
    ladder can rest.
  </p>
  <p>
    0.40 &gt; 0.289, so the ground can supply the 28.32 N required, and
    the ladder is in equilibrium.
  </p>
  <p>
    If instead <i>μ</i> = 0.20, then 0.20 &lt; 0.289, so friction cannot
    reach 28.32 N and the ladder slips. The required <i>F</i> does not
    change — only whether the ground can supply it.
  </p>
</section>
